tps://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/pylint-bot/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2015-10-31T09:59:44Z","updated_at":"2015-12-09T10:10:38Z","body":"_Original comment by_ **Claudiu Popa (BitBucket: [PCManticore](http://bitbucket.org/PCManticore), GitHub: @PCManticore)**:\n\n---\n\nIt should be fairly easy to do it. Your checker should implement visit_assign, which will be called for every assignment in your program. Pick from these only those which are valid for attribute docstrings, by verifying that their scope is a Class, a Module or `__init__`. Probably there are other places valid for attribute docstrings. The next thing you'll have to do is to get the next sibling of the assignment, by calling node.next_sibling() and verify that it is indeed a docstring. In pseudocode, this might look as this:\n\n```\n#!python\n\ndef visit_assign(self, node):\n    scope  = node.scope()\n    if not isinstance(scope, (Class, Module)):\n        return\n    sibling = node.next_sibling()\n    if isinstance(sibling, astroid.Expr) and isinstance(sibling.value, astroid.Const) and isinstance(sibling.value.value, six.string_types):\n        # this is attribute docstring\n        return\n   self.add_message(your_message, node=node)\n```\n\nLet me know if this works for your use case.\n","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/pylint-dev/pylint/issues/comments/163173555/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"pylint-bo